TypeScript adds types to JavaScript to avoid errors. Common primitive types: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, any.
- undefined: no value assigned.
- null: empty on purpose.
- any: no type safety.

Exemple :
let username: string = "Oumaima";
let age: number = 21;
let isStudent: boolean = true;

Arrays :
let numbers: number[] = [1, 2, 3];
let names: Array<string> = ["Ali", "Sara"];

Objects :
let user: {
  name: string;
  age: number;
} = {
  name: "Oumaima",
  age: 21
};

Union types allow a value to have multiple possible types. Useful when a value can change type.

Exemple :
let id: string | number;
id = 10;
id = "A123";

Type aliases let you name and reuse a type — cleaner and more maintainable code.

Exemple :
type User = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};
let admin: User = {
  name: "Admin",
  age: 30
};

Function with parameter and return types :
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

Function type (variable that holds a function) :
let calculate: (x: number, y: number) => number;
calculate = add;

Generics make code flexible and reusable; the type is decided when the function is called.

Exemple :
function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}
identity<string>("Hello");
identity<number>(10);

TypeScript adds access modifiers (public, private, protected) and typing to classes.

Exemple :
class Person {
  name: string;
  private age: number;
  constructor(name: string, age: number) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }
  greet() {
    console.log("Hello, " + this.name);
  }
}

Interfaces define the structure of an object. They are mainly used for objects and classes.

Exemple :
interface User {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  greet(): void;
}
let user1: User = {
  name: "Oumaima",
  age: 21,
  greet() {
    console.log("Hi");
  }
};
